{
  "fromId": "u",
  "toId": "o",
  "preamble": "The close back rounded vowel /u/ lowers to the close-mid back rounded vowel [o], a common vowel shift attested across many language families. It occurs in general vowel-system shifts, as in Tatar, where Proto-Turkic *u became the reduced vowel [ŏ] (*kum > qom 'sand'), and through a-mutation, as in Germanic (Proto-Germanic *hurną > Proto-West Germanic *horn). In Old Irish, short u was lowered to o (as i was to e) when the next syllable had, or once had, the low vowel a or o, as in cnocc 'hill' (Proto-Celtic *knukkos) against dative cnucc.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lowering, Chain Shift",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Tatar",
      "languageFamily": "Kipchak",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Turkic *kum 'sand'",
          "to": "ком (qom)",
          "note": "Proto-Turkic *u became a reduced vowel in Tatar and Bashkir; Tatar о is the reduced mid [ŏ]. Bashkir о, from the same *u, is written [ʊ̞] (ҡом [qʊ̞m]) and is filed under u → ʊ. Turkmen keeps u (gum, guş, gul)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Turkic *kuš 'bird'",
          "to": "кош"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Turkic *kul 'slave, servant'",
          "to": "кол"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Iroquoian to Proto-Northern-Iroquoian",
      "languageFamily": "Iroquoian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*-kuwa- 'big'",
          "to": "*-kowa-",
          "note": "PI *u lowers to *o in Northern Iroquoian, after PI *o had become *a"
